---

<DocsLayout title="The redesign" description="Choosing a Skill Rating engine and the Season Rank blueprint, with an interactive season simulator.">
  <h1>The redesign: one product, two numbers, two jobs</h1>
  <p class="subtitle">Hidden Skill Rating for fairness. Visible Season Rank for motivation. Don't cross the wires.</p>

  <p>
    Everything so far points at one conclusion, and Convocados' own ADR already states it:
    the failure wasn't a bad formula, it was asking <em>one</em> number to do <em>two</em>
    jobs. <a href="https://github.com/Cabeda/Convocados/blob/main/docs/adr/0031-seasonal-rank-ladder.md">ADR 0031</a>
    separates them — <strong>Skill Rating is hidden</strong> and drives balancing;
    <strong>Season Rank is visible</strong> and drives motivation. This page makes both
    concrete.
  </p>

  <table>
    <thead><tr><th></th><th>Skill Rating</th><th>Season Rank</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Job</td><td>Predict performance; balance teams</td><td>Make progress felt; give a goal</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Audience</td><td>Organizer, matchmaking</td><td>Every player</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Visibility</td><td>Hidden (admin only)</td><td>Visible to members</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Horizon</td><td>Lifetime; never resets</td><td>Per Season; soft-resets</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Engine</td><td>Glicko-2 / OpenSkill</td><td>Transparent point payouts</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Failure to avoid</td><td>Unfair teams</td><td>"+0 after a win"</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>1 · Choosing the Skill Rating engine</h2>
  <p>
    Convocados rates individuals whose teams are reshuffled weekly — that is the
    team-inference problem from <a href="/docs/rank/uncertainty">the uncertainty page</a>,
    where plain Elo is weakest (it applies a team result to each individual as if the team
    were one entity).
  </p>

  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Engine</th><th>Uncertainty</th><th>Individual from team result</th><th>Cost</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Elo (today)</td><td>No</td><td>Naive (winner takes all)</td><td>Lowest</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Glicko-2</td><td>Yes (RD, σ)</td><td>Per-player, head-to-head</td><td>Moderate</td></tr>
      <tr><td>OpenSkill / TrueSkill</td><td>Yes (Gaussian)</td><td>Yes — the point of it</td><td>Moderate–high</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <div class="callout callout-info">
    <strong>Recommendation.</strong> Move the hidden <strong>Skill Rating</strong> to
    <strong>OpenSkill</strong> (fallback: Glicko-2). It models 5-a-side team results and
    per-player uncertainty — the two things Elo fakes. Keep the change invisible: it only
    feeds balancing, so a bad model shows up as unfair teams, not as player confusion. Gate
    the migration behind a recompute so history stays consistent.
  </div>

  <h2>2 · The Season Rank blueprint</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Unit:</strong> Rank Points (RP), whole ladder ×10 — seeds, payouts, and tier
      edges all in RP, so the maths is unchanged but the numbers are worth chasing.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Payout:</strong> <code>K · (S − clamp(E, 0.1, 0.9))</code>. A win always pays
      at least <strong>+30 RP</strong>; even games pay most; upsets pay more.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Provisional:</strong> an unlock counter (not a hard 3-game cliff), with the
      fast-K window driven by evidence.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Seeding &amp; reset:</strong> seed from hidden Skill Rating; soft-reset 50%
      between Seasons so a Season is a fresh race without discarding skill.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Tiers:</strong> percentile edges frozen at calibration, expressed in RP.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Feedback:</strong> a per-game Rank reveal at score time (before → after,
      progress to next tier), plus the existing tier-transition modal.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>3 · Play a season</h2>
  <SeasonSimDemo client:visible />

  <p>
    Switch between <em>current</em> and <em>Rank Points</em> and play a few seasons. Current
    quietly pays the favourite almost nothing per win; Rank Points pays every game — which
    is the entire motivation argument, made measurable.
  </p>

  <h2>4 · Does it answer the two failures?</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>"Static Elo is demoralizing."</strong> Skill Rating is hidden; the public ladder
      is Season Rank, which soft-resets — everyone starts the race near each other, and
      improvement is visible per game.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>"Two numbers confuse players."</strong> Players see exactly one: Season Rank
      (plus the Crew ladder). Skill Rating is an organizer tool.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Residual risk to watch:</strong> a skill-anchored reset still favours stronger
      players over a Season. That is honest — the fix for feeling stuck is visible per-game
      progress and tiers, not equalising ability.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <details>
    <summary>The reason to run two systems rather than one is that they serve different…</summary>
    <p>Purposes and audiences. Two jobs — fair teams versus felt progress — pull in opposite directions.</p>
  </details>
  <details>
    <summary>OpenSkill suits Convocados' Skill Rating because teams are…</summary>
    <p>Reshuffled every week. Weekly reshuffles make the team a noisy observation of each player.</p>
  </details>
  <details>
    <summary>The visible Season Rank should never show a player…</summary>
    <p>A hidden Skill Rating. Skill Rating is hidden; exposing it recreates the static-number problem.</p>
  </details>

